IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Spent Convictions Act 1988 (Western Australia) -- what a conviction is (s 3); which convictions Part 2 does not reach
-- (s 4); serious and lesser convictions (ss 9, 10); the prescribed period (s 11, with s 32 for old convictions); how a
-- conviction becomes spent (s 6 by a District Court judge's order, s 7 by the Commissioner of Police's certificate, s 8 and
-- Schedule 2 for Queensland, Commonwealth and New South Wales convictions, and a Sentencing Act 1995 s 39 order); what else
-- Part 3 treats as spent (s 12); what being spent means: no duty to disclose (ss 25(2), 27), no reliance in other written
-- laws or character tests (ss 25(1), 26), no discrimination (ss 17-23, with s 24 for the remedy), no looking it up (s 28);
-- where that protection stops (ss 14, 15, and Schedule 3, the roles in which it does not apply); that a spent conviction is
-- not revived (s 30); and the hearing and costs of an application to a judge (Schedule 1 cll 1, 3, 7, 8).
--
-- SEAT: the person with an old matter. Every decision is phrased as that person would ask it: "What kind of conviction is
-- mine?", "When may I first apply?", "Is my conviction spent?", "Must I disclose it?", "Is it unlawful for them to hold it
-- against me?". Where the Act hands a judgment to someone else (the District Court judge's discretion in s 6(4), whether a
-- requirement "is not reasonable" in s 17(2)(b), whether a person has a "lawful reason" in s 28(1), the judge's view of
-- costs in Schedule 1 cl 8), the answer is conditional on that judgment, which is asked as a fact.
--
-- Provisions covered: s 3(1) ("conviction", "minor punishment", "spent conviction"), s 3(2)(a), (b), s 4, s 6(1), (2), (4)
-- (as a comment), s 7(1)-(3), s 8(1), s 9, s 10, s 11, s 12, s 14(1), (2), s 15, s 17(2), ss 18-23, s 24, s 25, s 26(1),
-- s 27, s 28(1), (2), s 30, s 32, Schedule 1 cll 1(1), 3(1), 7, 8; Schedule 2 cll 1-3; Schedule 3 cl 1(1) items 2-9 and
-- 11, cl 1(2), (3) (items 1, 4, 16), (5) (items 5, 6), (7), (9), (12), cl 2(1) (items 4, 6, 8), (2), (3), (4), (6) item 1,
-- cl 3(1) item 1.
--
-- Edition: compilation from legislation.wa.gov.au (mrdoc 49346, retrieved 7 September 2026), as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_49346.txt. The deposit names no compilation number. Its compilation table ends with
-- the Education and Care Services National Law Application Act 2026 Pt 6 Div 5 (in force 1 May 2026); the last reprint
-- listed is Reprint 7 as at 23 October 2015. The uncommenced provisions table lists the Evidence Act 2025 Pt 12 Div 19 (to
-- be proclaimed; not shown, not encoded). Dates taken from the compilation table: the Act (other than ss 1 and 2) commenced
-- 1 July 1992 (s 32); the Cannabis Law Reform Act 2010 Pt 4 commenced 1 August 2011 (s 11(6)(b)). The Spent Convictions
-- Regulations 1992 are not deposited: s 3 lets them prescribe a different "minor punishment" amount, and s 28(2) names
-- "prescribed" persons and laws. The $100 is the Act's own figure and is used; the s 28(2) prescription is an INPUT.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: the full Schedule 3 table (about 90 roles; the roles encoded are the ones an ordinary person most often meets,
-- and the role is an INPUT chosen from them); the extensions of Schedule 3 to the other side of each relationship (cl 1(2),
-- (4), (6), (8), (10), (11), (13), cl 2(4), (5)) except as the comments note; the Schedule 3 exceptions for review boards,
-- Department of Justice officers and the screening CEOs (cl 1(1) items 1-1B, 10-10B, 12-15, cl 2(7), (8), cl 3(2), (3));
-- whether a sentence imposed outside Western Australia corresponds to a WA sentence (s 3(2)(c); asked as the sentence);
-- whether a non-WA conviction is spent under its own law (Schedule 2; an INPUT); the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 procedure
-- the s 24 complaint uses; Part 4 machinery (s 29, s 31, s 33); and the Sentencing Act 1995 s 39 order itself (an INPUT).
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill, from the seat of the
-- individual to whom the law is applied, with the design defaults ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA)`

§§ `numbers and helpers`

-- s 11(1)(a): "10 years, or 3 years if subsection (6) applies".
`the ordinary period, in months` MEANS 120
`the cannabis period, in months` MEANS 36

-- s 9(a): "imprisonment for more than one year"; s 9(b): "a fine of $15 000 or more".
`the longest imprisonment that is not serious, in months` MEANS 12
`the smallest fine that is serious` MEANS 15000

-- s 3(1): "minor punishment means a fine not exceeding $100 or such amount as may be prescribed". No amount is used from
-- the regulations, which are not deposited.
`the largest minor fine` MEANS 100

-- s 6(2)(b): refused "within the preceding 2 years".
`the months a refusal bars a new application` MEANS 24

-- s 32 and the compilation table: "Act other than s. 1 and 2: 1 Jul 1992".
`the day the Act commenced` MEANS Date 1 7 1992

-- s 11(6)(b) and the compilation table: Cannabis Law Reform Act 2010 Pt 4, "1 Aug 2011".
`the day the cannabis reform commenced` MEANS Date 1 8 2011

-- s 28(1): "Penalty: $1 000."
`the fine for unlawfully obtaining a spent conviction` MEANS 1000

GIVEN `a` IS A DATE
      `b` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is on or after` MEANS Day `a` AT LEAST Day `b`

GIVEN `a` IS A DATE
      `b` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the later date of` MEANS IF `is on or after` `a` `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

GIVEN `x` IS A NUMBER
      `y` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the larger of` MEANS IF `x` AT LEAST `y` THEN `x` ELSE `y`

-- The same day of the month, a number of months later (Date rolls a month past 12 into the following years). A start on
-- the 29th to 31st of a month can roll into the next month; none arises here.
GIVEN `d` IS A DATE
      `n` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`months after` MEANS Date (DATE_DAY `d`) (DATE_MONTH `d` PLUS `n`) (DATE_YEAR `d`)

§§ `3, 4, 9, 10 -- what kind of conviction is mine`

-- s 3(1): "conviction means a conviction incurred by a natural person for an offence against the law of this State or of
-- a foreign country". A conviction under Commonwealth law or another State's or Territory's law is not a "conviction" for
-- ss 6 and 7; s 8 and Schedule 2 deal with it.
DECLARE `Where the conviction comes from` IS ONE OF
    `Western Australia`
    `a foreign country`
    `Queensland`
    `the Commonwealth or Norfolk Island`
    `New South Wales`
    `another State or Territory`

-- One conviction, as the person could describe it. s 3(2)(a): imprisonment "until a fine is paid" is not imprisonment;
-- leave it out of the months. s 3(2)(b): indefinite imprisonment and old Criminal Code detention are "indeterminate".
-- s 3(2)(c): a sentence imposed outside WA is read as the nearest WA sentence; state it that way.
DECLARE `My conviction` HAS
    `where it comes from`                                          IS A `Where the conviction comes from`
    `the date of conviction`                                       IS A DATE
    `the sentence includes life imprisonment`                      IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 4(2), s 9, s 10(1)
    `the imprisonment imposed, in months`                          IS A NUMBER   -- s 9(a), s 11(3)(b): imposed, not served
    `the imprisonment is for an indeterminate period`              IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 9(a), s 11(2)
    `the date I was discharged from the indeterminate sentence`    IS A DATE     -- s 11(2)(a)
    `the indeterminate imprisonment actually served, in months`    IS A NUMBER   -- s 11(2)(b)
    `the fine imposed, in dollars`                                 IS A NUMBER   -- s 9(b)
    `it is a cannabis offence of the kind in s 11(6)(a)`           IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 11(6)(a)
    `while it was unspent I was later given a serious sentence or life` IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 10(2)
    `a later conviction came while it was unspent`                 IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 11(4)
    `the date of the later conviction`                             IS A DATE     -- s 11(4)(a)(ii)
    `the later conviction carried a punishment other than a fine`  IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 11(5)
    `the later conviction's fine, in dollars`                      IS A NUMBER   -- s 11(5), s 3 "minor punishment"
    `the later conviction's prescribed period, in months`          IS A NUMBER   -- s 11(4)(a)(i)
    `the later imprisonment is cumulative on this one`             IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 11(4)(b)
    `the later imprisonment imposed, in months`                    IS A NUMBER   -- s 11(4)(b)
    `it is a youth conviction under Young Offenders Act 1994 s 189` IS A BOOLEAN -- s 4(1)(a)
    `it has stopped counting under Young Offenders Act 1994 s 189` IS A BOOLEAN  -- Young Offenders Act s 189(2), (9)
    `it was deemed not a conviction under an old Act`              IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 4(1)(b), (c), s 12(ab), (ac)
    `the Commissioner of Police has issued me a certificate that it is spent` IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 7(1)
    `a District Court judge has ordered that it is spent`          IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 6(1): a judgment
    `a judge has refused an order for it`                          IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 6(2)(b)
    `the date of the last refusal`                                 IS A DATE     -- s 6(2)(b)
    `a court made a spent conviction order under Sentencing Act 1995 s 39` IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 3 "spent conviction"
    `it is spent under its own jurisdiction's law`                 IS A BOOLEAN  -- Schedule 2
    `my parole or early release was later cancelled`               IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 30: read by nothing

-- s 4(2): life imprisonment "is not capable of becoming spent under section 6 or 7". s 9: "serious conviction means a
-- conviction in respect of which the sentence imposed is -- (a) imprisonment for more than one year or for an indeterminate
-- period; or (b) a fine of $15 000 or more." s 10(1): "lesser conviction" is anything else short of life. s 10(2): a
-- lesser conviction "that is not spent" becomes serious if a later conviction brings a s 9 sentence or life.
DECLARE `Kind of conviction` IS ONE OF
    `one that cannot become spent under s 6 or 7 (life imprisonment)`
    `a serious conviction`
    `a lesser conviction`

GIVEN `c` IS A `My conviction`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`its own sentence is serious` MEANS
       `c`'s `the imprisonment is for an indeterminate period`
    OR `c`'s `the imprisonment imposed, in months` GREATER THAN `the longest imprisonment that is not serious, in months`
    OR `c`'s `the fine imposed, in dollars` AT LEAST `the smallest fine that is serious`

@export What kind of conviction is mine under the Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA): serious, lesser, or one that can never be spent (ss 4(2), 9, 10)?
GIVEN `the conviction` IS A `My conviction` @desc Your conviction, its sentence and what has happened since
GIVETH A `Kind of conviction`
DECIDE `What kind of conviction is mine` IS
    BRANCH
      IF `the conviction`'s `the sentence includes life imprisonment` THEN `one that cannot become spent under s 6 or 7 (life imprisonment)`
      IF `its own sentence is serious` `the conviction` THEN `a serious conviction`
      IF `the conviction`'s `while it was unspent I was later given a serious sentence or life` THEN `a serious conviction`
      OTHERWISE `a lesser conviction`

§§ `11, 32 -- when the prescribed period expires`

-- s 11(1): "(a) 10 years, or 3 years if subsection (6) applies, plus any period of imprisonment relevant to that conviction".
-- (2): for an indeterminate period, "(a) the period of 10 years commences with the day on which the person is discharged
-- from that sentence; and (b) the period of imprisonment is the actual period served." (3): otherwise "(a) the period of
-- 10 years commences with the day on which the conviction is incurred; and (b) the period of imprisonment (if any) is the
-- period imposed, regardless of the period actually served." (6): 3 years for the listed cannabis offences if the
-- conviction "was not incurred before the commencement of the Cannabis Law Reform Act 2010 Part 4".
-- READING: (2) is read literally: the 10 years start at discharge and the time served is added on top.
GIVEN `c` IS A `My conviction`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the base period, in months` MEANS
    IF     `c`'s `it is a cannabis offence of the kind in s 11(6)(a)`
       AND `is on or after` (`c`'s `the date of conviction`) `the day the cannabis reform commenced`
    THEN `the cannabis period, in months`
    ELSE `the ordinary period, in months`

@export How long is the prescribed period for my conviction, in months, before any later conviction is counted (Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) s 11(1)-(3), (6))?
GIVEN `the conviction` IS A `My conviction` @desc Your conviction and its sentence
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How long is my prescribed period in months` IS
    IF `the conviction`'s `the imprisonment is for an indeterminate period`
    THEN `the base period, in months` `the conviction` PLUS `the conviction`'s `the indeterminate imprisonment actually served, in months`
    ELSE `the base period, in months` `the conviction` PLUS `the conviction`'s `the imprisonment imposed, in months`

GIVEN `c` IS A `My conviction`
GIVETH A DATE
`the day the period starts` MEANS
    IF `c`'s `the imprisonment is for an indeterminate period`
    THEN `c`'s `the date I was discharged from the indeterminate sentence`
    ELSE `c`'s `the date of conviction`

-- s 11(4): if, when a person incurs the "latest conviction", "he has any other conviction that is not a spent conviction",
-- the time already run is disregarded and the period for all of them is "the longer or longest of the prescribed periods"
-- and runs "from the time of the latest conviction"; (b) a cumulative sentence for the latest conviction "shall be added to
-- the prescribed period for that previous conviction". (5): the latest conviction "does not include a conviction for which
-- no punishment, or only minor punishment, was imposed".
-- READING: (b) is applied after (a): the cumulative term is added to the longer period.
GIVEN `c` IS A `My conviction`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a later conviction restarts the period` MEANS
        `c`'s `a later conviction came while it was unspent`
    AND (    `c`'s `the later conviction carried a punishment other than a fine`
         OR  `c`'s `the later conviction's fine, in dollars` GREATER THAN `the largest minor fine`)

-- s 32(2): for a conviction before 1 July 1992 the prescribed period expires "(a) on the commencement of this Act; or (b) on
-- the day on which it would have expired if this Act had been in force continuously ... whichever is the later."
@export From what day may I apply to have my conviction spent, once its prescribed period has expired (Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) ss 6(2)(a), 7(2), 11, 32)?
GIVEN `the conviction` IS A `My conviction` @desc Your conviction, its sentence and any later conviction while it was unspent
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `From what day may I apply` IS
    `the later date of` `the day the Act commenced`
        (IF `a later conviction restarts the period` `the conviction`
         THEN `months after` (`the conviction`'s `the date of the later conviction`)
                 (    `the larger of` (`How long is my prescribed period in months` `the conviction`) (`the conviction`'s `the later conviction's prescribed period, in months`)
                  PLUS (IF `the conviction`'s `the later imprisonment is cumulative on this one` THEN `the conviction`'s `the later imprisonment imposed, in months` ELSE 0))
         ELSE `months after` (`the day the period starts` `the conviction`) (`How long is my prescribed period in months` `the conviction`))

§§ `6, 7, 8 -- how my conviction becomes spent`

-- s 6(1): "A serious conviction ... becomes spent if, on application being made by that person to a District Court judge,
-- the judge makes an order declaring that the conviction is spent." (2): not "(a) until the prescribed period for that
-- conviction has expired; or (b) if a judge has refused to make an order ... within the preceding 2 years." (4): the order
-- "is at the discretion of the judge", having regard to the sentence, the time since, whether it stops the applicant
-- working, the applicant's circumstances, the offence, and "whether there is any public interest to be served in not
-- making an order". s 7(1): "A lesser conviction ... becomes spent when, on application being made in the prescribed form
-- ... to the Commissioner of Police, the Commissioner issues to the applicant a certificate". (3): "The Commissioner of
-- Police does not have a discretion ... but must issue a certificate if the application conforms with this Act."
-- s 4(1): ss 6 and 7 do not apply to a youth conviction under Young Offenders Act 1994 s 189, or to a conviction deemed
-- not to be a conviction under the Offenders Community Corrections Act 1963 s 20 or the Child Welfare Act 1947 s 40(2).
-- s 8(1) and Schedule 2: a Queensland, Commonwealth (or Norfolk Island) or New South Wales conviction is spent if spent
-- under that jurisdiction's own law. Schedule 2 names no other State or Territory.
DECLARE `How my conviction can become spent` IS ONE OF
    `not under Part 2: a youth conviction stops counting under Young Offenders Act 1994 s 189`
    `not under Part 2: it was deemed not a conviction, and Part 3 already applies (s 12)`
    `never under s 6 or 7: the sentence included life imprisonment`
    `only under its own jurisdiction's law, which Schedule 2 recognises`
    `not at all under this Act: Schedule 2 names no law of that State or Territory`
    `not yet: the prescribed period has not expired`
    `not yet: a judge refused an order within the last 2 years`
    `apply to the Commissioner of Police, who must issue a certificate (s 7)`
    `apply to a District Court judge, who decides at discretion (s 6)`

GIVEN `c` IS A `My conviction`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`it is a WA or foreign conviction` MEANS
    CONSIDER `c`'s `where it comes from`
    WHEN `Western Australia` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a foreign country` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `c` IS A `My conviction`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Schedule 2 names its jurisdiction` MEANS
    CONSIDER `c`'s `where it comes from`
    WHEN `Queensland` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the Commonwealth or Norfolk Island` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `New South Wales` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `c` IS A `My conviction`
      `on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a refusal still bars me` MEANS
        `c`'s `a judge has refused an order for it`
    AND (NOT `is on or after` `on` (`months after` (`c`'s `the date of the last refusal`) `the months a refusal bars a new application`))

@export How can my conviction become spent, and may I apply now (Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) ss 4, 6, 7, 8 and Schedule 2)?
GIVEN `the conviction` IS A `My conviction` @desc Your conviction, its sentence and what has happened since
      `on` IS A DATE @desc The day you would apply
GIVETH A `How my conviction can become spent`
DECIDE `How can my conviction become spent` IS
    BRANCH
      IF `the conviction`'s `it is a youth conviction under Young Offenders Act 1994 s 189` THEN `not under Part 2: a youth conviction stops counting under Young Offenders Act 1994 s 189`
      IF `the conviction`'s `it was deemed not a conviction under an old Act` THEN `not under Part 2: it was deemed not a conviction, and Part 3 already applies (s 12)`
      IF `Schedule 2 names its jurisdiction` `the conviction` THEN `only under its own jurisdiction's law, which Schedule 2 recognises`
      IF (NOT `it is a WA or foreign conviction` `the conviction`) THEN `not at all under this Act: Schedule 2 names no law of that State or Territory`
      IF `the conviction`'s `the sentence includes life imprisonment` THEN `never under s 6 or 7: the sentence included life imprisonment`
      IF (NOT `is on or after` `on` (`From what day may I apply` `the conviction`)) THEN `not yet: the prescribed period has not expired`
      IF `What kind of conviction is mine` `the conviction` EQUALS `a lesser conviction` THEN `apply to the Commissioner of Police, who must issue a certificate (s 7)`
      IF `a refusal still bars me` `the conviction` `on` THEN `not yet: a judge refused an order within the last 2 years`
      OTHERWISE `apply to a District Court judge, who decides at discretion (s 6)`

-- s 3(1): "spent conviction means a conviction that is spent under section 6, 7 or 8 or that is spent by virtue of a spent
-- conviction order made under section 39 of the Sentencing Act 1995." A lesser conviction is not spent merely because 10
-- years have passed: s 7(1) makes it spent "when ... the Commissioner issues ... a certificate".
-- s 30: a spent conviction "is not revived" when the person is later held not to have been discharged because parole or
-- early release was cancelled. So the field for a cancelled release is read by nothing.
GIVEN `c` IS A `My conviction`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`it is spent under s 6 or 7` MEANS
        (NOT `c`'s `it is a youth conviction under Young Offenders Act 1994 s 189`)
    AND (NOT `c`'s `it was deemed not a conviction under an old Act`)
    AND (    (`What kind of conviction is mine` `c` EQUALS `a lesser conviction` AND `c`'s `the Commissioner of Police has issued me a certificate that it is spent`)
         OR  (`What kind of conviction is mine` `c` EQUALS `a serious conviction` AND `c`'s `a District Court judge has ordered that it is spent`))

@export Is my conviction spent under the Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) (ss 3, 6, 7, 8, 30 and Schedule 2)?
GIVEN `the conviction` IS A `My conviction` @desc Your conviction and what has happened since (certificate, court order, or its own jurisdiction's law)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my conviction spent` IS
    CONSIDER `the conviction`'s `where it comes from`
    WHEN `Western Australia` THEN
             `the conviction`'s `a court made a spent conviction order under Sentencing Act 1995 s 39`
          OR `it is spent under s 6 or 7` `the conviction`
    WHEN `a foreign country` THEN `it is spent under s 6 or 7` `the conviction`
    WHEN `another State or Territory` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE `the conviction`'s `it is spent under its own jurisdiction's law`

§§ `12 -- what Part 3 acts on`

-- s 12: Part 3 applies to "(a) a dismissal under ... section 669(1)(a) of The Criminal Code; and ... section 34 or 34B of
-- the Child Welfare Act 1947; (ab), (ac)" the old deemed non-convictions, "and (b) a charge formally made in court that a
-- person has committed an offence where -- (i) the charge is withdrawn; or (ii) the charge is disposed of without a
-- conviction being recorded, as if the dismissal or charge were a spent conviction." Young Offenders Act 1994 s 189(9)
-- does the same for a youth conviction that has stopped counting. Nothing in the Act reaches a matter that never produced
-- a charge in court (a caution or a juvenile justice team): there is no conviction and no charge for Part 3 to act on.
DECLARE `How my matter ended` IS ONE OF
    `with a conviction`
    `a charge in court withdrawn`
    `a charge in court disposed of without a conviction being recorded`
    `a dismissal under The Criminal Code s 669(1)(a) or the Child Welfare Act 1947 s 34 or 34B`
    `without any charge in court (no action, a caution, or a juvenile justice team)`

-- Schedule 3 cl 2(2): the offences that matter for the child-protection exceptions. (3): except for Chapter XXII and
-- Chapter XXXI, the offence counts only if "the person in respect of whom the offence was committed was under 18".
-- (2)(b), (c): a corresponding foreign or Schedule 2 offence counts as the WA offence it corresponds to.
DECLARE `The kind of offence` IS ONE OF
    `an offence against morality (Criminal Code Chapter XXII)`
    `a sexual offence (Criminal Code Chapter XXXI)`
    `homicide, endangering life or health, assault, an offence against liberty, child stealing or desertion of children`
    `any other offence`

-- One matter: one charge or conviction, as the person could describe it.
DECLARE `My matter` HAS
    `how it ended`                                IS A `How my matter ended`
    `the conviction`                              IS A `My conviction`   -- read only if it ended with a conviction
    `the kind of offence`                         IS A `The kind of offence`
    `the person offended against was under 18`    IS A BOOLEAN

DECLARE `What Part 3 acts on` IS ONE OF
    `a spent conviction`
    `a youth conviction treated as spent (Young Offenders Act 1994 s 189(9))`
    `a conviction deemed not a conviction under an old Act, treated as spent (s 12)`
    `a dismissal or a charge without a conviction, treated as spent (s 12)`
    `nothing yet: the conviction is not spent`
    `nothing: no conviction and no charge in court`

@export What in my matter does Part 3 of the Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) protect (ss 3, 12; Young Offenders Act 1994 s 189(9))?
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `My matter` @desc How your matter ended, and the conviction if there was one
GIVETH A `What Part 3 acts on`
DECIDE `What does Part 3 protect in my matter` IS
    CONSIDER `the matter`'s `how it ended`
    WHEN `with a conviction` THEN
        BRANCH
          IF `Is my conviction spent` (`the matter`'s `the conviction`) THEN `a spent conviction`
          IF `the matter`'s `the conviction`'s `it has stopped counting under Young Offenders Act 1994 s 189` THEN `a youth conviction treated as spent (Young Offenders Act 1994 s 189(9))`
          IF `the matter`'s `the conviction`'s `it was deemed not a conviction under an old Act` THEN `a conviction deemed not a conviction under an old Act, treated as spent (s 12)`
          OTHERWISE `nothing yet: the conviction is not spent`
    WHEN `without any charge in court (no action, a caution, or a juvenile justice team)` THEN `nothing: no conviction and no charge in court`
    OTHERWISE `a dismissal or a charge without a conviction, treated as spent (s 12)`

GIVEN `m` IS A `My matter`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Part 3 protects my matter` MEANS
    CONSIDER `What does Part 3 protect in my matter` `m`
    WHEN `nothing yet: the conviction is not spent` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `nothing: no conviction and no charge in court` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `16, Schedule 3 -- the roles in which the protection does not apply`

-- s 16 and Schedule 3: "The persons specified in the first column ... are excepted from the provisions of Part 3 specified
-- in the second column". cl 1 is "in respect of all spent convictions"; cl 2(1) only "in respect of a spent conviction for
-- an offence referred to in subclause (2)". The exceptions extend to the other side of the relationship: the employer,
-- licensing authority or appointer (cl 1(2), (4), (6), (8), (10), (13); cl 2(4)). So in an excepted role, the question
-- from the employer or authority is not protected either.
DECLARE `My role` IS ONE OF
    `none of the Schedule 3 roles`
    `being considered for appointment as a Justice of the Peace`                                     -- cl 1(1) item 2
    `a police officer, police auxiliary officer or Aboriginal police liaison officer, or applicant`  -- cl 1(1) item 3
    `a special constable or police cadet, or applicant`                                             -- cl 1(1) item 4
    `a prison officer or applicant, or a high-level security work permit under the Prisons Act 1981` -- cl 1(1) item 5
    `employment by the Gold Corporation`                                                            -- cl 1(1) item 6
    `a casino key employee or casino employee licence`                                              -- cl 1(1) item 7
    `a licence or permit under the Security and Related Activities (Control) Act 1996`              -- cl 1(1) item 8
    `a licence, permit or approval under the Firearms Act 2024`                                     -- cl 1(1) item 9
    `a Public Transport Authority security officer`                                                 -- cl 1(1) item 11
    `employment in a registered school or the Department of Education`                             -- cl 1(3) items 1, 4
    `registration as a teacher under the Teacher Registration Act 2012`                             -- cl 1(3) item 16
    `a licence to provide a child care service under the Child Care Services Act 2007`              -- cl 1(5) item 5
    `assessment for adoptive parenthood under the Adoption Act 1994 s 38(1)`                        -- cl 1(5) item 6
    `employment in the Department of Health, the Mental Health Commission or a health service provider` -- cl 1(7)
    `employment in an organisation funded by the Disability Services Commission`                    -- cl 1(9) item 5
    `admission to the legal profession, or practice as a lawyer`                                    -- cl 1(12)
    `employment in a care centre, preschool centre or child care service`                           -- cl 2(1) item 4
    `the safety house scheme`                                                                       -- cl 2(1) item 6
    `employment in the Department of Sport and Recreation`                                          -- cl 2(1) item 8
    `a working with children check (Working with Children (Screening) Act 2004 s 34)`              -- cl 2(6) item 1
    `NDIS worker screening (National Disability Insurance Scheme (Worker Screening) Act 2020 s 33)` -- cl 3(1) item 1

DECLARE `Schedule 3 exception` HAS
    `s 18 excepted`                    IS A BOOLEAN
    `s 19 excepted`                    IS A BOOLEAN
    `s 20 excepted`                    IS A BOOLEAN
    `s 22 excepted`                    IS A BOOLEAN
    `Division 4 excepted`              IS A BOOLEAN
    `ss 27 and 28 excepted`            IS A BOOLEAN
    `only for the cl 2(2) offences`    IS A BOOLEAN

-- The second column of Schedule 3, row by row, for the roles encoded.
GIVEN `the role` IS A `My role`
GIVETH A `Schedule 3 exception`
`what Schedule 3 excepts for` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the role`
    --                                                                                                        s 18   s 19   s 20   s 22   Div 4  27-28  cl 2 only
    WHEN `none of the Schedule 3 roles` THEN                                                    `Schedule 3 exception` OF FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE
    WHEN `being considered for appointment as a Justice of the Peace` THEN                      `Schedule 3 exception` OF FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, TRUE,  FALSE, FALSE
    WHEN `a police officer, police auxiliary officer or Aboriginal police liaison officer, or applicant` THEN `Schedule 3 exception` OF TRUE,  FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, TRUE,  FALSE, FALSE
    WHEN `a special constable or police cadet, or applicant` THEN                               `Schedule 3 exception` OF FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, TRUE,  FALSE, FALSE
    WHEN `a prison officer or applicant, or a high-level security work permit under the Prisons Act 1981` THEN `Schedule 3 exception` OF TRUE,  TRUE,  TRUE,  TRUE,  TRUE,  FALSE, FALSE
    WHEN `employment by the Gold Corporation` THEN                                              `Schedule 3 exception` OF TRUE,  FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, TRUE,  FALSE, FALSE
    WHEN `a casino key employee or casino employee licence` THEN                                `Schedule 3 exception` OF FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, TRUE,  TRUE,  FALSE, FALSE
    WHEN `a licence or permit under the Security and Related Activities (Control) Act 1996` THEN `Schedule 3 exception` OF FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, TRUE,  TRUE,  FALSE, FALSE
    WHEN `a licence, permit or approval under the Firearms Act 2024` THEN                       `Schedule 3 exception` OF FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, TRUE,  FALSE, FALSE
    WHEN `a Public Transport Authority security officer` THEN                                   `Schedule 3 exception` OF TRUE,  FALSE, TRUE,  TRUE,  TRUE,  FALSE, FALSE
    WHEN `employment in a registered school or the Department of Education` THEN               `Schedule 3 exception` OF TRUE,  TRUE,  TRUE,  TRUE,  TRUE,  FALSE, FALSE
    WHEN `registration as a teacher under the Teacher Registration Act 2012` THEN               `Schedule 3 exception` OF TRUE,  TRUE,  TRUE,  TRUE,  TRUE,  FALSE, FALSE
    WHEN `a licence to provide a child care service under the Child Care Services Act 2007` THEN `Schedule 3 exception` OF TRUE,  FALSE, TRUE,  TRUE,  TRUE,  FALSE, FALSE
    WHEN `assessment for adoptive parenthood under the Adoption Act 1994 s 38(1)` THEN          `Schedule 3 exception` OF FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, TRUE,  FALSE, FALSE
    WHEN `employment in the Department of Health, the Mental Health Commission or a health service provider` THEN `Schedule 3 exception` OF TRUE,  TRUE,  TRUE,  TRUE,  TRUE,  FALSE, FALSE
    WHEN `employment in an organisation funded by the Disability Services Commission` THEN      `Schedule 3 exception` OF TRUE,  TRUE,  TRUE,  TRUE,  TRUE,  FALSE, FALSE
    WHEN `admission to the legal profession, or practice as a lawyer` THEN                      `Schedule 3 exception` OF FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, TRUE,  TRUE,  FALSE, FALSE
    WHEN `employment in a care centre, preschool centre or child care service` THEN             `Schedule 3 exception` OF TRUE,  FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, TRUE,  FALSE, TRUE
    WHEN `the safety house scheme` THEN                                                         `Schedule 3 exception` OF FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, TRUE,  FALSE, TRUE
    WHEN `employment in the Department of Sport and Recreation` THEN                            `Schedule 3 exception` OF TRUE,  FALSE, TRUE,  FALSE, TRUE,  FALSE, TRUE
    WHEN `a working with children check (Working with Children (Screening) Act 2004 s 34)` THEN `Schedule 3 exception` OF FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, TRUE,  FALSE
    WHEN `NDIS worker screening (National Disability Insurance Scheme (Worker Screening) Act 2020 s 33)` THEN `Schedule 3 exception` OF FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, TRUE,  FALSE

-- Schedule 3 cl 2(2), (3): the child-protection offences.
GIVEN `m` IS A `My matter`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`it is a child-protection offence` MEANS
    CONSIDER `m`'s `the kind of offence`
    WHEN `an offence against morality (Criminal Code Chapter XXII)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a sexual offence (Criminal Code Chapter XXXI)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `homicide, endangering life or health, assault, an offence against liberty, child stealing or desertion of children` THEN `m`'s `the person offended against was under 18`
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `m` IS A `My matter`
      `the role` IS A `My role`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Schedule 3 reaches my matter` MEANS
       (NOT (`what Schedule 3 excepts for` `the role`)'s `only for the cl 2(2) offences`)
    OR `it is a child-protection offence` `m`

GIVEN `m` IS A `My matter`
      `the role` IS A `My role`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Schedule 3 lifts Division 4 for me` MEANS
    (`what Schedule 3 excepts for` `the role`)'s `Division 4 excepted` AND `Schedule 3 reaches my matter` `m` `the role`

GIVEN `m` IS A `My matter`
      `the role` IS A `My role`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Schedule 3 lifts ss 27 and 28 for me` MEANS
       `Schedule 3 lifts Division 4 for me` `m` `the role`
    OR ((`what Schedule 3 excepts for` `the role`)'s `ss 27 and 28 excepted` AND `Schedule 3 reaches my matter` `m` `the role`)

§§ `14, 15, 25(2), 27 -- must I disclose it`

-- s 27(1): "Questions about a convicted person put to that person or any other person shall not be taken to relate to a
-- spent conviction or the charge to which the conviction relates." (2): a rule of common law or equity, or "a provision of
-- an agreement or arrangement", "does not require the disclosure". s 25(2): "A written law of this State that requires a
-- person to disclose ... does not require the disclosure or acknowledgment of a spent conviction". s 14(1)(a), (2)(a):
-- ss 25(2), 26(1) and 27 do not apply in "proceedings of a court or tribunal that applies the laws of evidence or
-- proceedings under section 6". s 15: nor "for the purposes of any decision relating to the bail of a person".
-- Schedule 1 cl 1(1)(a): an application to the judge "shall set out -- (a) all previous convictions".
-- Schedule 3: an excepted role loses Division 4 (ss 25-28) or, for the screening roles, ss 27 and 28 only; s 25(2) then
-- still applies to them.
DECLARE `Where the question comes from` IS ONE OF
    `a question put to me, or to someone else about me`
    `a written law of Western Australia that requires disclosure`
    `an agreement, an arrangement, or a rule of common law or equity`
    `a court or tribunal that applies the laws of evidence`
    `a decision about my bail`
    `my own application to a District Court judge under s 6`

DECLARE `Must I disclose` IS ONE OF
    `nothing for this Act to act on: no conviction and no charge in court`
    `this Act does not excuse it: the conviction is not spent`
    `yes: the protection does not apply before a court or tribunal that applies the laws of evidence (s 14)`
    `yes: the protection does not apply to a bail decision (s 15)`
    `yes: the application must set out all previous convictions (Schedule 1 cl 1; s 14)`
    `yes: Schedule 3 excepts my role from this protection`
    `no: the question is taken not to relate to it (s 27(1))`
    `no: the written law does not require it (s 25(2))`
    `no: the agreement or rule does not require it (s 27(2))`

@export Must I disclose my old matter when asked, or does the Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) let me answer as if it never happened (ss 14, 15, 25(2), 27, Schedule 3)?
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `My matter` @desc How your matter ended, the conviction if any, and the kind of offence
      `my role` IS A `My role` @desc The job, licence or role the question is asked for, if it is one of the Schedule 3 roles; otherwise none of the Schedule 3 roles
      `where it comes from` IS A `Where the question comes from` @desc Who or what is asking: a question, a written law, an agreement, a court, a bail decision, or your own application to the judge
GIVETH A `Must I disclose`
DECIDE `Must I disclose it` IS
    BRANCH
      IF `What does Part 3 protect in my matter` `the matter` EQUALS `nothing: no conviction and no charge in court` THEN `nothing for this Act to act on: no conviction and no charge in court`
      IF (NOT `Part 3 protects my matter` `the matter`) THEN `this Act does not excuse it: the conviction is not spent`
      IF `where it comes from` EQUALS `a court or tribunal that applies the laws of evidence` THEN `yes: the protection does not apply before a court or tribunal that applies the laws of evidence (s 14)`
      IF `where it comes from` EQUALS `a decision about my bail` THEN `yes: the protection does not apply to a bail decision (s 15)`
      IF `where it comes from` EQUALS `my own application to a District Court judge under s 6` THEN `yes: the application must set out all previous convictions (Schedule 1 cl 1; s 14)`
      IF `where it comes from` EQUALS `a written law of Western Australia that requires disclosure` THEN
          IF `Schedule 3 lifts Division 4 for me` `the matter` `my role`
          THEN `yes: Schedule 3 excepts my role from this protection`
          ELSE `no: the written law does not require it (s 25(2))`
      IF `Schedule 3 lifts ss 27 and 28 for me` `the matter` `my role` THEN `yes: Schedule 3 excepts my role from this protection`
      IF `where it comes from` EQUALS `a question put to me, or to someone else about me` THEN `no: the question is taken not to relate to it (s 27(1))`
      OTHERWISE `no: the agreement or rule does not require it (s 27(2))`

§§ `14, 15, 25(1), 26 -- may it count against me under a law of Western Australia`

-- s 25(1): "A reference in a written law of this State (other than this Act) to a conviction of a person for an offence
-- does not include a reference to a spent conviction." s 26(1): a person assessing "the good character, fitness, propriety
-- or other like attribute of a person" under a written law "shall not ... have regard to a spent conviction or the charge".
-- (2): failing to comply "is not an offence". s 14(2)(b): s 25(1) does not apply in a court deciding guilt "where a
-- conviction is relevant" or "the appropriate punishment". s 15: nor for bail.
DECLARE `How a law of Western Australia uses my record` IS ONE OF
    `a reference in a written law to a conviction`
    `an assessment of my good character, fitness or propriety`
    `a court deciding my guilt, where a conviction is relevant, or my punishment`
    `a decision about my bail`

@export May my old matter count against me under a law of Western Australia, such as a licence's good character test (Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) ss 14, 15, 25(1), 26, Schedule 3)?
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `My matter` @desc How your matter ended, the conviction if any, and the kind of offence
      `my role` IS A `My role` @desc The job, licence or role concerned, if it is one of the Schedule 3 roles; otherwise none of the Schedule 3 roles
      `how the law uses it` IS A `How a law of Western Australia uses my record` @desc How the law would use your record: a reference to convictions, a character or fitness test, a court on guilt or punishment, or bail
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May it count against me under a law of Western Australia` IF
        (NOT `Part 3 protects my matter` `the matter`)
    OR  `how the law uses it` EQUALS `a court deciding my guilt, where a conviction is relevant, or my punishment`
    OR  `how the law uses it` EQUALS `a decision about my bail`
    OR  `Schedule 3 lifts Division 4 for me` `the matter` `my role`

§§ `17-24 -- is it unlawful to discriminate against me over it`

-- s 17(2): a person discriminates "on the ground of a spent conviction" if "(a) on the ground of that conviction or the
-- charge to which it relates, the discriminator treats the aggrieved person less favourably than ... a person who had never
-- incurred a conviction; or (b) ... requires the aggrieved person to comply with a requirement or condition that is not
-- reasonable having regard to the circumstances of the case." ss 18-23 make it unlawful for an employer (s 18), a
-- principal of a commission agent (s 19) or of a contract worker (s 20), an organisation of employees or employers (s 21),
-- an authority conferring an authorisation or qualification for a profession, trade or occupation (s 22), and an
-- employment agency (s 23). s 24(1): the remedy is a complaint "under section 83(1) or (2) of the Equal Opportunity Act
-- 1984"; (2): no other sanction, "criminal or civil"; (3): defamation is not affected. Schedule 3 never excepts ss 21 or 23.
DECLARE `Who is treating me less favourably` IS ONE OF
    `an employer, when I apply for a job or as an employee (s 18)`
    `a principal engaging me as a commission agent (s 19)`
    `a principal for whom I work as a contract worker (s 20)`
    `an organisation of employees or of employers (s 21)`
    `an authority that confers a licence or qualification for my occupation (s 22)`
    `an employment agency (s 23)`
    `someone else, such as a landlord or a shop`

@export Is it unlawful for them to treat me less favourably because of my old matter (Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) ss 17-24, Schedule 3)?
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `My matter` @desc How your matter ended, the conviction if any, and the kind of offence
      `my role` IS A `My role` @desc The job, licence or role concerned, if it is one of the Schedule 3 roles; otherwise none of the Schedule 3 roles
      `who` IS A `Who is treating me less favourably` @desc Who is treating you less favourably
      `they treat me less favourably because of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they treat you less favourably than someone never convicted, because of the conviction or charge
      `they impose a requirement on me that is not reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they impose a requirement or condition that is not reasonable in the circumstances (a judgment for the Equal Opportunity Commission or Tribunal)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is it unlawful for them to discriminate against me over it` IF
        `Part 3 protects my matter` `the matter`
    AND (`they treat me less favourably because of it` OR `they impose a requirement on me that is not reasonable`)
    AND `the Division covers them` `who`
    WHERE
        `ex` MEANS `what Schedule 3 excepts for` `my role`
        `reached` MEANS `Schedule 3 reaches my matter` `the matter` `my role`
        GIVEN `w` IS A `Who is treating me less favourably`
        GIVETH A BOOLEAN
        `the Division covers them` `w` MEANS
            CONSIDER `w`
            WHEN `an employer, when I apply for a job or as an employee (s 18)` THEN NOT (`ex`'s `s 18 excepted` AND `reached`)
            WHEN `a principal engaging me as a commission agent (s 19)` THEN NOT (`ex`'s `s 19 excepted` AND `reached`)
            WHEN `a principal for whom I work as a contract worker (s 20)` THEN NOT (`ex`'s `s 20 excepted` AND `reached`)
            WHEN `an organisation of employees or of employers (s 21)` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `an authority that confers a licence or qualification for my occupation (s 22)` THEN NOT (`ex`'s `s 22 excepted` AND `reached`)
            WHEN `an employment agency (s 23)` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `28 -- may someone look it up`

-- s 28(1): "A person shall not, without lawful reason, obtain information about a spent conviction, or the charge to which
-- the conviction relates, from an official criminal record. Penalty: $1 000." (2): not "a prescribed person" obtaining it
-- under "a prescribed law of the Commonwealth, another State or a Territory" about a person who works or seeks to work
-- "with a child or a person with disability". Who and what is prescribed is in regulations not deposited: an INPUT.
@export May someone obtain my old matter from an official criminal record (Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) s 28, Schedule 3)?
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `My matter` @desc How your matter ended, the conviction if any, and the kind of offence
      `my role` IS A `My role` @desc The job, licence, check or role concerned, if it is one of the Schedule 3 roles; otherwise none of the Schedule 3 roles
      `they have a lawful reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person looking has a lawful reason to obtain it
      `they are prescribed and screening me for work with children or people with disability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person is prescribed under s 28(2) and obtains it under a prescribed screening law of another Australian jurisdiction
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May someone obtain it from an official criminal record` IF
        (NOT `Part 3 protects my matter` `the matter`)
    OR  `they have a lawful reason`
    OR  `they are prescribed and screening me for work with children or people with disability`
    OR  `Schedule 3 lifts ss 27 and 28 for me` `the matter` `my role`

@export What is the most a person can be fined for obtaining my spent conviction from an official criminal record without lawful reason (Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) s 28(1))?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most someone can be fined for looking it up` IS `the fine for unlawfully obtaining a spent conviction`

§§ `Schedule 1 -- my application to a District Court judge`

-- Schedule 1 cl 3(1): "The hearing shall be in private unless -- (a) the applicant requests that the hearing be in public;
-- or (b) the judge considers that, in the circumstances of the case, the hearing should be in public."
@export Will my application to a District Court judge to have a serious conviction spent be heard in private (Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) Schedule 1 cl 3(1))?
GIVEN `I ask for a public hearing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you ask for the hearing to be in public
      `the judge considers it should be public` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the judge considers the hearing should be in public
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Will my hearing be in private` IF
    (NOT `I ask for a public hearing`) AND (NOT `the judge considers it should be public`)

-- Schedule 1 cl 8(1): "each party to an application shall bear his own costs", except (2) where the judge "(a) refuses to
-- make an order as mentioned in clause 7(a)" (vexatious, misconceived or lacking in substance, without a hearing) "or (b)
-- is of the opinion that the circumstances justify doing so". The Commissioner of Police is a party (cl 2(1)).
@export Can I be ordered to pay costs on my application to a District Court judge (Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) Schedule 1 cll 7, 8)?
GIVEN `the judge refused it without a hearing as vexatious, misconceived or lacking in substance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the judge refused your application under Schedule 1 cl 7(a)
      `the judge thinks the circumstances justify costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the judge is of the opinion that the circumstances justify awarding costs
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I be ordered to pay costs` IF
       `the judge refused it without a hearing as vexatious, misconceived or lacking in substance`
    OR `the judge thinks the circumstances justify costs`
